A||||9||phillip||spun out||||1||
Z||000000||phillip||04-24-2000||10:33 AM||||<BR>i have my "spare wheels" on the back of my '87 4cyl. (cavalier Z-24 16X6 with stock eagle rsa's 205/55-16). light rain last night going around a corner, didn't think i was going that fast, next thing i know the car is compleatly sideways.<P>i don't think it was because of jumping back on the gas, the car dosen't have that much power anyway. maybe some thing spilled on the road? i've never got the impression that the car would let go under regular cirumstances,but now i'm a little more wary of it. didn't hit anything by the way, but i ended up facing the opposite direction, kinda scary being the passenger in the drivers seat.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||04-24-2000||10:58 AM||||This may sound too simple but, check to make sure the tires aren't "directionally" inhibited. I once bought an 84 Corvette and didn't really pay too much attention to the tires other than to see how deep the treads were. One day after a heavy rain, I was crusing down I-4 about 60 MPH and hit a puddle in the road. The car started into a spin and suddenly I was in for a free ride.<P>After the 360, I slowed down and got it home. When I checked the tires the next day I realized the rear tires had been rotated and the rotation arrows were facing the wrong way. This caused water to be trapped under the tires and hydroplane.<P>Something to look at,<BR>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick's Dad||04-24-2000||11:44 AM||||Fun, huh? On dry pavement, I would be surprised, because my '85 2m4, with BFG Euro T/A 205/60-14's, practically went sideways for me (Keeping me facing straight) when I was cut off on an off ramp. I whipped my steering wheel left, then right, and it seemed my little car twisted itself to squeeze between the car that had cut me off and the one that was gaining on us on the left (It was two lanes). the next thing I know, I'm breathing heavy, travelling forward 50+MPH, and without a scratch. I gained a lot of respect for my little "commuter car" right there.<P>On a wet road, I don't put anything past my liitle girl. She's an 88 Formula with some little bolt ons here and there (And the A/C removed), and 225/60 15 BFG Comp T/A HR4's on the back and 205/60 15's up front, and I can easily spin them from a stop light. In fact, I have to be very careful with them when turning. I have faced the other way a couple of times. To be fair, for fun I throw the rear end out occasionally on dry as well, but it's more progressive and controllable than some say it is (Maybe it's the '88 suspension and the KYB's).<P>Also, I have never had a car (Except, maybe my first) where I didn't swap out the Goodyears for BFG's. The stick was noticeably better.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||04-24-2000||11:51 AM||||First, If it rains lightly after being dry for a few days, you need to be careful because with all the cars on the road some drip oil. What happens during a light rain the oil residue on the road is lifted by the rain and the road becomes slick. Water and oil do not mix together and the oil floats on top of the water. You will actually have better traction after a heavy rain since the oil residue is washed off the road. The second thing is, you said the eagle tires you have are stock. These eagle tires are a hard compound tire. They work best when they are warmed up. On a cool rainy day they can become slick too. I would suggest getting a softer compund performance tire. Michellin, dunlop, BF goodrich, etc all make very good performance tires that are way better than the eagles. I personally don't care for the goodyear eagles. I know I may get flamed for bashing goodyear tires but I have driven many cars with different tires and have never liked the handling or performance of the goodyear eagles.<BR>I have BFGoodrich 235/60R14's on my car now and they work great.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||sully5||04-24-2000||12:02 PM||||Mine spun on me going to work a couple of weeks ago. This was right after a rain, and was the first time I had driven it on wet roads. Fortunately there is not much traffic here at 630 in the am, so nothing got hurt. Quite an exciting little adventure. Of course I would never do anything like that on purpose, even in my old 350 Camaro. I too prefer the BFG's. That is what is on the little car now, and what I always ran on the old Camaro. Even got a set of the all terrains on the 4x4.||||reg||14
Z||000005||BN Boomer||04-24-2000||01:18 PM||||Well, I spun out at 65mph on I-5 just north of Seattle about a month ago while on my way work to one dark, rainy night. As you can see, the results were not quite as benign as those of the individuals above. I figure the concrete barrier that jumped into my path as I was going backwards at nearly road speed had something to do with it. Fortunately, I walked away with only minor aches and pains. <P>Hmmm...Was there ever a short wheelbase Fiero?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/boomers_fiero_files/fiero5.jpg"> <P>I think that next alignment is going to be pretty tough...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/boomers_fiero_files/fiero7.jpg"> <P>This is about the best shot available of what's left of my poor old 87 Coupe. Hey, what that in the background? Is there hope yet?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/boomers_fiero_files/fiero11.jpg">  ||||reg||9
Z||000006||Dan86gt3.8||04-24-2000||05:35 PM||||Well spinnig out in the fiero.  I have had alott of experience with that.  When it was a stock gt and it was my dads I spun it at least twice if not three times while dry and couple when wet.  the little guy sticks like mad when its dry but you put a little moisture on the road and he's all over the place.  Like I was going around one of thoose curved right turns where you yield if anyones coming, or if its clear you just whip on around and was just driving, and had slown down for the rain factor but all of a sudde I was facing backwards.  But I have to admitt spinnig it when its dry is a feat for me.  My best is when I did almosta complete 360 when I tried to make a corner at 60 I had did it at 50 before, but I figured if I swung out wide enough and cut across the apex and aceelerated out of it I would do it just fine but it didn't, go figure  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Anyways its never really scary cause the fiero never rocks or act like it wants to flip it just spins and stops beofre you really ever knew what happened.  I guess its the mid engine factor.  Anyways I am glad to see others have spun their fieros.  HOw about you ssca guys, I know yall got to spin in practice allthe time.  Or if yall aren't then you are not pushing the envelope enough and need to try harder.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000007||theogre||04-24-2000||08:01 PM||||This kind of thing is one of the reasons I try to replace tires below third to half tread. <P>Any number of stupid things can make a car loop. Tires on in the wrong spin direction is a real good one. shallow tread is this biggest cause. improper inflation will do it too. Under inflation especially, it let's the middle of the tread bow up and seals in allot more water.<P>Tires may be legal to 3/32 inch but I won't run 'em like that anymore on anything. No room for even a little water to escape.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ed||04-24-2000||11:37 PM||||Also watch out for "drop throttle oversteer"||||reg||1
Z||000009||Dan||04-25-2000||09:00 PM|||| Onlt run good tires on a Fiero unless yopu like spinning. The car was designed in Detroit with an understeer built in, which means instead of flipping or oversteering it will spin out, the safer of the choices.What ever tire you buy, make sure it has a high traction rating.I'm running Contra 2000 Contentenials,and just came thru the mountains at excessive speeds on cool rainy asphault roads having a great time.Also make sure your bushings,suspension and ball joints are in good condition.||||reg||1
